[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation/target/release/driver        # after: cd translation && cargo build --release
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both are driven as subprocesses with the same bytes on stdin; stdout, stderr and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit status (including death by signal) are compared. See
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/differential.rs` and `tests/common/mod.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE` was ignored, so the Rust program survived a write the C program died on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Status:** fixed in `src/main.rs` (`reset_sigpipe`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe whose read end has already been
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | stdout | stderr | exit status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | empty | empty | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before) | empty | empty | exit 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` during runtime
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m startup, before `main` is entered. The failing `write` therefore returned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `EPIPE` instead of terminating the process, and `print_foo` discards the write
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result (`let _ = write!(...)`), so the program ran to a normal `return 0`. The C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program keeps the default disposition and is killed by the signal.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Restore `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE` as the first statement of `main`, by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m declaring `signal(2)` with `extern "C"` — the standard library already links the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m platform C library, so this needs no new dependency.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Covered by:** `dying_from_sigpipe_when_stdout_has_no_reader`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Note.** This is the only difference found. It is not reachable through stdin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m content; it only appears when the consumer of stdout goes away. A harness that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reads the child's stdout to completion will never see it, which is why it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m survived the input-driven cases.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviour deliberately preserved, including the parts that look like bugs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m None of these required a change — the translation already matched — but each was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed against the C binary rather than assumed, and each is pinned by a test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A failed `scanf` leaves its destination alone.** `x`, `y`, `b` and `z` are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   initialised to `0` and `main` ignores every `scanf` return value, so a failed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   conversion silently yields `0`. Empty input prints `0 0 0 0` and exits 0; there
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is no error message and no non-zero exit anywhere in this program.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` reads across newlines.** Leading whitespace — including `\n`, `\r`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `\t`, `\v` and `\f` — is skipped by every numeric directive, so `"1\n2\n3\n4"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is read exactly like `"1 2 3 4"`. This is the `scanf`/`fgets` distinction and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   it is what the C does.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A matching failure poisons every later conversion.** The byte that stopped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the scan is pushed back into the stream, so it stops the next directive too.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"1 2 x 4"` prints `1 2 0 0`, not `1 2 0 4` — the `4` is never reached. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Rust `Scanner` reproduces this with a one-byte pushback.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **An accepted sign is consumed even when the conversion then fails.** For
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"--1 2 3 4"` the first `%u` eats the first `-`, fails on the second, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   pushes only that second `-` back; the next `%u` then reads `-1`. Result:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0 7 1 3`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%u` accepts a sign and wraps.** glibc converts with `strtoul`, so `-1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   becomes `ULONG_MAX`, truncates to `0xFFFFFFFF`, and the 2-bit field prints `3`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-18446744073709551615` wraps to `1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Out-of-range values saturate at 64 bits, then truncate to 32.** `%u`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   saturates to `ULONG_MAX`; `%d` saturates to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`. So
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"0 0 0 99999999999999999999999"` prints `-1` (`LONG_MAX` truncated to `int`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and the negative counterpart prints `0` (`LONG_MIN` truncated to `int`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **In-range-for-64-bit values truncate rather than saturate.** `4294967296` for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `%u` gives `0`; `2147483648` for `%d` gives `-2147483648`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Bit-field widths are masks, not errors.** `unsigned int x : 2` keeps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `x & 0x3`, `unsigned int y : 3` keeps `y & 0x7`; `7 15 …` prints `3 7 …`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`!!b` and `bool b : 1`.** Any non-zero `b` prints `1`, including values that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   are non-zero only before truncation. `%d` on the promoted one-bit `bool`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   prints `0` or `1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **No `%i`, so no hex.** `"0x10"` converts as `0` and leaves `x` in the stream,
